
ESTIMATED SCHEDULE FOR GEMDOS AS REQUESTED BY LT 2/22/91

MILESTONES:

	Getting Started:

		Modify GEMDOS so it compiles with either Alcyon CC or GCC
		(i.e. a compiler with good code generation and strict(er)
		type checking).  Run this GEMDOS and see that it works.

	Revise GEMDOS data structures, retaining compatibility:

		Implement a handle system that doesn't fall apart when the
		same file is open twice or referenced by two handles,
		possibly in different processes.

		Implement a file and directory system that does not rely on
		having the disk structure in memory taking up pool.

		Implement a process system that allows for multiple
		processes, where chasing 'run' up to the top is not
		adequate to visit all processes.

	Memory Manager: 

		Use TPA to keep track of TPA, rather than using pool.

	Devices:

		Ability to install devices by name at GEMDOS level and/or
		by number at BIOS level.  Some kind of Ioctl() better than
		Bconmap.

	Multi-Tasking:

		Simplest multi-tasking kernel.  Includes busy-waiting or
		blocking console I/O and a "concurrent" mode for Pexec.

		Better multi-tasking kernel. Definitely blocking console 
		I/O, priority levels ("nice"), possibly other stuff.

	AES and VDI:

		I was instructed to submit a schedule for a multi-tasking
		GEMDOS, not a multi-tasking TOS.  However, some features 
		for other levels of the OS can go in with minimal effort. 
		For instance, a semaphore on trap 2 can be created to stop
		multi-tasking while "in" the AES or VDI.  Switching while
		in the VDI isn't profitable anyway, and switching while in
		the AES would probably be a disaster.  With just a little
		help from Derek we can probably get something working where
		the AES uses block and unblock calls to allow and disallow
		the running of processes which are under its control, and
		keep the world safe for wind_update that way.  Processes
		which are NOT under AES's control would multi-task
		continuously, doing their daemon things or whatever.

	Interprocess Considerations:

		Record locking, and new open modes for file sharing/locking.
		MS-DOS semantics for calls (even when it's stupid.)

		Interprocess communication streams (pipe, named pipe, pty).

		Signals: at least block, unblock for AES to use, probably
		more complete, including TSTOP (^Z), CONT, INT (^C), QUIT
		(^\), KILL (-9), ALRM, CHLD, TTIN/TTOU (console read/write
		when you don't own the console), etc.

		Process data structure access (at least "ps" and maybe
		enough for a debugger).

		Memory protection.

		Virtual memory.

	Networking:

		A network device driver (for interrupts) plus a daemon
		process (for the OS interface layer) can run a peer-to-peer
		network in this environment.  The redirecting abilities
		of MetaDOS should probably exist inside GEMDOS so it
		stays current with new calls, etc.

SCHEDULE:

Getting Started is done.  Revised GEMDOS data structures sections 1 and 2
are done and under test, and part 3 is not much of a departure from the
current stuff.  Estimated time: 3 weeks.

The new memory manager is optional, and you lose compatibility and
reliability if you use it.  Furthermore, you have to go to yet another
system when you add memory protection and/or virtual memory.  It is
therefore not actually desirable.

The first kind of Multi-Tasking and the whole section labelled Interprocess
Considerations is what MiNT already does.  I don't always like what he's
done, like using "drive letters" Q: for queues (named pipes), V: for
devices, and X: for processes, but they are not totally unreasonable
solutions, and the rest appears to be really useful.

At a rough guess, I will say six weeks to steal MiNT's ideas and fold them
into GEMDOS. MiNT appears well-written and I have a good relationship with
the guy who wrote it, and fast turnaround on mail with him.  If we do use a
substantial part of it, it might be nice to throw him a bone, in the form
of a couple of thousand dollars.  Being Canadian, he would probably even
settle for Canadian dollars!

Using MiNT seems to me to be a good solution to getting something with lots
of desirable (read: Unix-like) features running in little time. There are
large quantities of MiNT which I would have to implement anyway. In other
words, "If MiNT did not already exist, it would be necessary to invent it."

SUMMARY

I estimate three more weeks from 2/25 to get the new-and-improved GEMDOS
with cleaner and brighter data structures up and running.

Six weeks after that to fold MiNT's ideas (and code!) into GEMDOS. This
last number is much the weaker of the two.

Good-case (not best, not worst) estimate, then, is the end of May for
something that works reasonably well adn is reasonably full-featured, but
hasn't undergone strenuous testing.  This basically assumes nothing but a
porting effort for the MiNT stuff, rather than reimplementation.
